One common way improper use of Java APIs leads to vulnerabilities is through **Unsafe Deserialization**. When an application uses `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` on untrusted data provided by a user, an attacker can inject malicious serialized objects to execute arbitrary code (Remote Code Execution).

The following example demonstrates this vulnerability and how to fix it using a look-ahead pattern to validate classes before they are instantiated.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class DeserializationVulnerability {

    // This method is vulnerable because it accepts any object type from the stream
    public void unsafeDeserialize(InputStream inputStream) throws Exception {
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(inputStream);

        // This line is vulnerable to Remote Code Execution (RCE) via gadget chains
        Object obj = ois.readObject(); 

        System.out.println("Deserialized object: " + obj.toString());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Simulating an incoming stream from a web request
        byte[] data = { /* malicious payload bytes */ };
        DeserializationVulnerability demo = new DeserializationVulnerability();
        demo.unsafeDeserialize(new ByteArrayInputStream(data));
    }
}
```

